[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm happy to announce the release of further packages for <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex\">Hex</a>, the computer algebra library for Lean. (<a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Hex%3A.20a.20computational.20algebra.20library/near/608173688\">initial announcement</a>; most recently <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Hex.3A.20graph.20isomorphism.20via.20a.20formalization.20of.20.60nauty.60/near/621968237\">graph isomorphism via a formalization of <code>nauty</code></a>, also written up <a href=\"https://kim-em.github.io/blog/2026-9-5-certified-graph-isomorphism-in-lean/\">on my blog</a>.) The <a href=\"https://kim-em.github.io/hex-dev/\">Hex manual</a> documents every released library.</p>\n<p>Today we have exact algebraic numbers, and arithmetic and factorization in number fields.</p>",
        "id": 622699726,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1788917667
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To get started, add to your <code>lakefile.toml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"TOML\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">[[require]]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"hex\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">git</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex.git\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">rev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"main\"</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and then:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hex</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hex</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- An algebraic number is a root of an integer polynomial, named by an</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- approximation: the nearest root wins.</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ZPoly.rootNear</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ZPoly.rootNear</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- All the roots at once, real ones first in increasing order:</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ZPoly.algebraicRoots</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Arithmetic:</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- the minimal polynomial of √2 + √3</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Equality (without importing the Mathlib theory library,</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- you need to use `==`, but with it `=` is allowed too):</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s2</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: ZPoly.rootNear #p[1, 0, -10, 0, 1] 3.146264369 -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- If you're working in a fixed number field, use `QAdjoin`: the field ℚ(a)</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- of a canonical number, whose elements are rational polynomials in it.</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cbrt2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ZPoly.rootNear</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">26</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">QAdjoin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cbrt2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cbrt2.toQAdjoin</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Arithmetic and equality are efficient in `QAdjoin`,</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- and inverses are calculated with extended GCDs.</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Elements print as the expression that rebuilds them: the generating</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- number and the coordinates. Here `c⁵ = 2c²`.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: QAdjoin.ofCoeffs (ZPoly.rootNear #p[-2, 0, 0, 1] 1.25992) #p[0, 0, 2] -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hex.NumberTower</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `NumberTower.rat` is ℚ. `adjoin T a` extends the tower `T` by a root `a`;</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- the result carries the new tower, the adjoined generator `gen`, and the</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- inclusion `embed` of `T`, which is why its type names `T`.</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Extension</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NumberTower.rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adjoin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NumberTower.rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s2.toRoot</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Extension</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">adjoin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3.toRoot</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">quartic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NumberTower</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Poly</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">liftZPoly</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Over ℚ(√2) the quartic splits into two quadratics; over ℚ(√2, √3) into</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- four linear factors. A factorization pairs each factor with its</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- multiplicity; here we read off the degrees.</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">factor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">quartic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.tower</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factors.map</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g.size</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">factor</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">quartic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.tower</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factors.map</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g.size</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `flatten T : Flattening T` is the primitive element theorem as a function:</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--   structure Flattening (T : NumberTower) where</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--     root          : AlgebraicNumber</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--     toPrimitive   : Elem T → QAdjoin root</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--     fromPrimitive : QAdjoin root → Elem T</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- i.e. a single algebraic number generating the whole tower, with the</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- coordinate changes between the tower and ℚ(root). Here root is ±√2 ± √3,</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- and √2 = (root³ − 9·root)/2.</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Flattening</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">flatten</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.tower</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F.root.p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F.toPrimitive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Q23.embed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.gen</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">coeffs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">9</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Arithmetic in a tower is coordinate arithmetic: a power of √2 + √3 costs a</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- few multiplications of coordinate vectors, over the basis 1, √2, √3, √6.</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Elem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.tower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.embed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q2.gen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Q23.gen</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">coeffs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">47525</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">19402</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- 47525 + 19402·√6</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- The same power as an `AlgebraicNumber` recomputes a minimal polynomial at</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- every step, and agrees. Its minimal polynomial is only quadratic, since the</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- power lies in ℚ(√6).</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F.toPrimitive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toAlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: ZPoly.rootNear #p[1, -95050, 1] 95049.99998947 -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The library <code>HexNumberFieldMathlib</code> proves these computations correct.<br>\nWe can also use it to interpret each <code>AlgebraicNumber</code> as a complex number,<br>\nand show that this interpretation is injective and preserves the arithmetic:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HexNumberFieldMathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Hex</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a.toComplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b.toComplex</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber.toComplex_injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toComplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a.toComplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b.toComplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">AlgebraicNumber.add_toComplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>(These examples are drawn from the Hex manual pages for <a href=\"https://kim-em.github.io/hex-dev/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&amp;name=hex-number-field\"><code>HexNumberField</code></a> and <a href=\"https://kim-em.github.io/hex-dev/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&amp;name=hex-number-field-tower\"><code>HexNumberFieldTower</code></a>.)</p>",
        "id": 622699762,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1788917688
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's quite a lot going on here.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex-number-field\"><code>HexNumberField</code></a> has three representations of an algebraic number, and you mostly only see the first:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>AlgebraicNumber</code>, the canonical form: a selected root of its irreducible minimal polynomial, with decidable equality.</li>\n<li><code>AlgebraicRoot</code>, the lazy form underneath it. Adding two algebraic numbers builds a resultant, whose roots include the sum, and isolates the right one; it factors down to the minimal polynomial only when you ask, or when an equality has to be decided.</li>\n<li><code>QAdjoin a</code>, an element of the single field <code>ℚ(a)</code>, stored as a rational polynomial in <code>a</code>. Arithmetic is polynomial arithmetic modulo the minimal polynomial, with no root isolation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can also find the roots of a polynomial whose coefficients are themselves algebraic numbers.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex-number-field-tower\"><code>HexNumberFieldTower</code></a> is the other library. It computes in a tower <code>ℚ(α₁, …, αₙ)</code>, built one generator at a time: arithmetic, factorization by Trager's algorithm one level at a time, adjoining further roots, splitting fields, and primitive-element flattening.</p>\n<p>Both come with Mathlib companions that prove the executable code correct, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex-number-field-mathlib\"><code>HexNumberFieldMathlib</code></a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/hex-number-field-tower-mathlib\"><code>HexNumberFieldTowerMathlib</code></a>: <code>AlgebraicNumber</code> gets a <code>Field</code> instance whose operations are <em>definitionally</em> the executable ones, the interpretation into <code>ℂ</code> is injective, and every tower operation has a soundness theorem and a completeness theorem (the <code>Option</code>-valued form never returns <code>none</code> on valid input). Everything is kernel-checkable; nothing uses <code>native_decide</code>.</p>\n<p>At present the performance situation is decent, but far from state of the art. Fixed-field multiplication and inversion in <code>ℚ(2^{1/n})</code> are within a factor of about 1.5 of PARI's <code>t_POLMOD</code> arithmetic at degree 16. Factoring over a number field is a different matter: our Trager factorization of <code>Xⁿ − X − 1</code> over <code>ℚ(√2)</code> runs 20 to 50 times slower than PARI's <code>nffactor</code>, and the gap widens with the degree. PARI is fast and <code>nffactor</code> has had decades of attention, so I expect to narrow that rather than close it. The <a href=\"https://kim-em.github.io/hex-dev/\">manual pages</a> have some comparison tables.</p>",
        "id": 622699818,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1788917721
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As usual for Hex, these libraries are split into purely computational libraries that do not depend on Mathlib, plus associated libraries that make the connection with Mathlib theory. In the medium term I am interested in merging the \"with Mathlib\" libraries into Mathlib itself. Unreleased libraries, and all future development, live at <a href=\"https://github.com/kim-em/hex-dev\">github.com/kim-em/hex-dev</a>. Contributions and pull requests are welcome, but specs must be updated <strong>before</strong> any new features or substantial changes, as separately reviewed PRs.</p>",
        "id": 622699851,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1788917740
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Discussion can continue at <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"482893\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/482893-Computer-algebra/topic/Discussion.3A.20Hex/with/621676269\">#Computer algebra &gt; Discussion: Hex</a>. I'd be very happy to have user reports, particularly on the ergonomics of <code>AlgebraicNumber</code> (what else should be a one-liner?).</p>",
        "id": 622699898,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1788917771
    }
